## Chapter 12: The Secret Forge of Vault 3
The midnight air inside Vault 3 was thick with the scent of hot iron and burning sulfur.
Deep behind the main sorting bays, past rows of rusted machinery, lay a recessed chamber hidden behind a heavy hydraulic blast door. There were no municipal surveillance lenses mounted on these concrete walls. No automated System sensors logged the tools on the workbench.
It was Master Silas’s private workshop.
Kaelen stood over an iron anvil table, his shirt sleeves rolled up to his elbows. Sweat glinted on his forearms, catching the flickering amber glow of a high-temperature plasma torch clamped to a side arm.
In front of him lay the raw materials he had harvested from his midnight hunts: a long slab of jet-black **Acid-Vein Matriarch** chitin, three dense bone-plates from the **Iron-Claw Raptor**, and a cluster of shattered **Grave-Crawler** carapace.
Silas sat on a rusted stool in the corner, a half-empty mug of bitter black coffee in his hand, his brass magnifying goggles resting on his forehead. He watched in complete silence as Kaelen worked.
Kaelen didn't use a standard welder's mask. His **[Low-Light Spatial Perception]** and **40 Vitality** allowed his eyes to adjust to the blinding plasma arc without flinching.
He picked up a heavy pneumatic grinding tool. With swift, terrifyingly precise movements, he shaved down the outer edges of the Raptor bone-plates. He wasn't building a giant, showy chest piece or a glowing broadsword like the elite cadets from Ironclad.
He was building **concealed sub-dermal bracing**.
Two narrow, ultra-dense forearm guards designed to fit under his frayed uniform sleeves, and a pair of reinforced inner insoles for his worn leather boots.
"You're making them thin," Silas noted raspsily, taking a sip of his coffee. "Thin plate doesn't stop a kinetic warhammer."
"It does if the internal weave is layered correctly," Kaelen replied smoothly, his voice calm and even.
He touched the hot chitin plate with his bare thumb. Using a controlled micro-pulse of his **24 Aetheric Resonance**, he mapped the internal mineral grain of the material. He aligned the Raptor bone's parallel shock-absorbent fibers directly against the Matriarch chitin's acid-resistant density, fusing them together under five hundred degrees of focused plasma heat.
*CLANG. CLANG. CLANG.*
Kaelen brought a short-handled ball-peen hammer down on the hot alloy. He didn't use a heavy sledgehammer; he used short, compact strikes driven by his **38 Strength**. Every blow forced the molecular structure of the materials to lock together without creating visible stress fractures.
Silas watched the hammer impacts, his eyes narrowing.
*He's hitting that plate with enough force to dent an armor-plating, but he isn't deforming the edges,* Silas thought, his tongue clicking behind his teeth. *The kid's controlling his kinetic transfer down to the millimeter.*
After forty minutes of continuous work, Kaelen quenched the newly forged guards in a tub of stale cooling oil. A thick cloud of white steam hissed into the air, smelling sharply of iron and burnt marrow.
Kaelen pulled the guards out with heavy iron tongs.
They were masterpiece pieces of dark, matte-grey armor—lightweight, thin as a coin, but practically indestructible to standard physical impacts.
He picked up a jar of dull, grey industrial corrosion-paint that Silas kept on the bottom shelf. Using a rough bristle brush, Kaelen slathered the high-density bracers in thick, ugly coats of the cheap paint, intentionally scratching the surfaces with a piece of coarse sandpaper.
When he was finished, the high-density alloy looked like two worthless pieces of rusted scrap metal scavenged from a factory dumpster.
"Scratched rust-paint," Silas let out a short, dry laugh, shaking his head. "You really are a paranoid bastard, Vance."
"If it looks expensive, people ask where I got the credits," Kaelen said, strapping the dull grey bracers around his forearms beneath his shirt.
The bracers fitted snugly against his skin, perfectly flush. When he pulled his worn uniform sleeves down, there was zero visible bulge. If an instructor or a scanning drone looked at him, all they would see were his usual skinny wrists and dirty cuffs.
He slipped the reinforced insoles into his boots. Instantly, his footwork felt locked to the floor—capable of absorbing massive downward kinetic shocks if he ever needed to land from high drops or deliver a high-velocity **[Kinetic Overdrive]** strike.
Kaelen stood up, flexing his fingers.
The hidden gear added zero weight to his **40 Agility**, but provided an invisible layer of physical protection that would turn a lethal blow from an elite cadet into a minor inconvenience.
Silas tossed a heavy canvas bag onto the workbench. Inside were three small, unlabelled glass vials filled with a murky, amber liquid.
"What's this?" Kaelen asked.
"Low-grade stamina tonic," Silas muttered, turning back to his work. "Standard issue for Anvil Ridge's draft team. It's garbage compared to the essence potions Gideon Prep drinks, but if you drink one during the evaluation, it gives you a believable excuse for why a 'frail' Salvager isn't collapsing from exhaustion after three hours of running."
Kaelen looked at the old mechanic. The gesture was simple, but tactical. It gave Kaelen a public cover for his immense **40 Vitality**.
Kaelen picked up the bag and slung it over his shoulder. "Thank you, Master Silas."
"Don't thank me," Silas rasped, adjusting his brass goggles. "Just make sure when you're out in that mock-breach zone with Renna's squad, you don't let those Ironclad kids push you into a corner. They fight like wild dogs. You hit them before they bite."
Kaelen offered a slow, cold nod. "They won't even see the strike coming."
He stepped out of the hidden workshop, slipping through Vault 3's unmonitored back exit into the freezing night air of the courtyard, his hidden armor humming quietly against his skin.
